Subject: [PATCH 3/3] R80b, part 1, patch 3 (cld-advisor, at main's word in
 M14; Review 80, a reader's finding F10, its second part, WHICH MAIN HAS NOT
 CHECKED: it was made again by the builder before it was mended): A TEXT THAT
 OPENS WITH PUNCTUATION IS NOT CUT DOWN TO THE MARK ALONE.

THE FINDING, MADE AGAIN on the commit before. The builder's function that cuts a sentence for a table's cell cuts at the last space within its length. A text that opens with punctuation and a space, and then holds a word longer than the cut, was cut at that first space. Five cases: a comma, then a long word gives the mark alone; so does a colon, a space, a long word and more words; so does punctuation alone, longer than the cut; a dash or an opening bracket, then a long word, gives the one character and the mark. A dash followed by ordinary words is cut at a word's end, as it should be.

WHAT IS CHANGED. Where the cut keeps no letter and no digit, the text is cut at its length in characters, as a single long word already is. The old line stands in a dated note.

THE CASES are in the lock's own test of the function: the five, and the twin, whose result is the same before and after. With the builder's file of the commit before, that test fails on the first of the five.

No cell of the plan's page changes: the builder's output on this commit is the page of the commit before. The lock passes: 19 tests. 0 of 19.
